{
  "annotations": [
    {
      "start_time": 0,
      "end_time": 0,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Montreal Poet Michel Lefebvre, dressed as a space alien, is quite visibly and audibly tipsy during the performance, knocking over vials of liquid on a table, with a girl in a Hugo Ball costume hovering robotically nearby."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "https://www.discogs.com/release/2341164-Various-Ultimatum",
              "link": "https://www.discogs.com/release/2341164-Various-Ultimatum"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Event Description"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "93331003-0020-4d99-9696-2a108516f266"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 108,
      "end_time": 108,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Michel Lefebvre later founded and ran TOPO – a Montreal center for multimedia digital expression in the arts - for 25 years."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Biographical Context"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "bb9e1d7f-741c-4ffe-9dff-f8a0b6255754"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 0,
      "end_time": 0,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "For his performance—called “Poésie guerrière”—Montrealer Jack V starts off by singing while shaving with an electric razor."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Event Description"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "c5096707-baf3-4ae1-bfc3-43a7b6586b37"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 61,
      "end_time": 61,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "He says \"sept ans d'malheur!\" (seven years bad luck!) and smashes a mirror"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Event Description"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "10e49b31-1787-4793-8115-5db62be58dc6"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 89,
      "end_time": 89,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The music we hear faintly in the background is the song Hospitality by Sparks"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Event Description"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "b4f6c26f-ea60-4cea-8ece-4d3271f30b78"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 156,
      "end_time": 156,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "He then stripped down to the waist, thus revealing the war paint on his body. He started dancing, prancing and swaying in abandon like a wild man."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Event Description"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "777aa0bc-d449-450c-b7e6-e11ee6ec5383"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 275,
      "end_time": 275,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Jack was a member of the Neoists, and played bass in Monty Cantsin’s First Aid Brigade."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Biographical Context"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "2a072d36-3223-4c97-a5b5-2248591effbf"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 431,
      "end_time": 431,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Festival participants Jack V, Jean-Luc Bonspiel, Jean-Martin Mignault and I later formed Montreal’s seminal agitrock band Vent du Mont Schärr (1986 – 1990)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Biographical Context"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "ecb5a3c7-8893-44da-91ee-53c4647e5fd4"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1008,
      "end_time": 1008,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "He is joined by a regiment of a half-dozen drummers, one by one—a Post-Punk marching band—martially rat-tatting their snares. Jack was feline and menacing, irresistibly magnetic. This was poetry beyond words, and one of the festival’s highlights."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Event Description"
        },
